272
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:17,840
the tissue paper, the tags, the

273
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:20,400
barcodes, everything is replicated down

274
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:23,160
to the detail. These sell for 100 to

275
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:25,560
$200. And these are the ones that cause

276
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:27,200
real problems because these are the ones

277
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:29,520
that can pass through authentication

278
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:31,360
systems designed specifically to catch

279
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:32,960
them. The economics of this grading

280
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:34,920
system are fascinating if you think

281
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:36,680
about it for a second. The factory

282
00:10:34,920 --> 00:10:38,960
doesn't have to choose one tier and

283
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:41,160
commit to it. A single workshop can

284
00:10:38,960 --> 00:10:42,760
produce shoes at every level, adjusting

285
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:44,640
the materials and labor depending on

286
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:46,480
what the buyer orders. The same way a

287
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:48,680
restaurant has dishes at different price

288
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:50,600
points on the same menu. Low tier for

289
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:53,080
the budget market, mid tier for the

290
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:55,800
casual buyer, top tier for the resale

291
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:57,400
market. Each tier has its own margin.

292
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:59,520
And the margin at every level is

293
00:10:57,400 --> 00:11:01,480
enormous compared to what it costs to

294
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:03,640
actually make the shoe. What happens

295
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:05,640
after the shoes leave the factory is

296
00:11:03,640 --> 00:11:07,760
where the operation stops looking like a

297
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:10,600
small time hustle and starts looking

298
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:12,960
like a Fortune 500 supply chain. Getting

299
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:15,080
22 shipping containers of fake Jordans

300
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:18,080
from Putian to a self storage unit in

301
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:20,320
Brooklyn is not something you improvise.

302
00:11:18,080 --> 00:11:22,480
It requires the same kind of logistics

303
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:24,640
infrastructure that legitimate importers

304
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:26,760
use because it literally runs through

305
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:28,440
the same ports, the same customs

306
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:30,720
checkpoints, and the same freight

307
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:32,640
carriers. The best documented case of

308
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:35,200
how this works is the federal complaint

309
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:37,120
against a man named Ching Fu Zeng, a

310
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:39,800
Chinese national who operated under the

311
00:11:37,120 --> 00:11:42,040
alias Ray. Zeng ran a counterfeiting

312
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:46,320
network that, over the course of several

313
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:48,440
years, moved an estimated $472 million

314
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:50,880
worth of fake Nike and Louis Vuitton

315
00:11:48,440 --> 00:11:52,760
sneakers into the United States. The

316
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:55,640
investigation that eventually caught him

317
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:57,320
began in 2012, and when federal agents

318
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:59,480
finally arrested him at Dulles

319
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:02,400
International Airport, they had tracked

320
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:04,440
at least 22 40-ft shipping containers

321
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:06,520
tied to his operation. Here's how it

322
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:08,920
worked. Zeng and his network set up

323
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:10,520
fictitious company names, registered

324
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:13,160
them with authentic employer

325
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:14,800
identification numbers, the tax IDs that

326
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:17,640
legitimate businesses used to clear

327
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:19,080
customs, and built an entire paper trail

328
00:12:17,640 --> 00:12:21,440
designed to make the shipments look

329
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:23,360
routine. The customs paperwork listed

330
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:25,840
the contents as everyday commercial

331
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:27,760
goods. The containers shipped from China

332
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:29,680
into either the Port of New York or the

333
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:32,080
Port of New Jersey, where they cleared

334
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:33,600
customs and were released. From there,

335
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:35,720
the containers were transported to

336
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:38,400
self-storage facilities across Brooklyn,

337
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:40,080
Queens, and Long Island. Inside those

338
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:42,400
units, the shoes were broken into

339
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:44,320
smaller quantities, repackaged, and

340
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:46,720
distributed to wholesalers and

341
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:48,160
retailers. The whole operation ran on

342
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:49,920
burner phones and email addresses

343
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:52,240
disguised to look like they belonged to

344
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:54,839
real companies. Federal investigators

345
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:57,200
actually let five of the containers pass

346
00:12:54,839 --> 00:12:59,400
through customs on purpose, just to

347
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:01,280
track where they ended up and map the

348
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:04,240
distribution network before making

349
00:13:01,280 --> 00:13:06,800
arrests. That's the large-scale method,

350
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:08,480
shipping containers into major ports.

351
00:13:06,800 --> 00:13:10,760
But, there's a second method that until

352
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:13,080
very recently was arguably even harder

353
00:13:10,760 --> 00:13:15,320
to stop, and it exploited a loophole

354
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:18,520
that almost nobody outside of trade law

355
00:13:15,320 --> 00:13:21,000
knew about. It's called the Section 321

356
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:24,920
de minimis exemption, and it works like

357
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:27,640
this. Under US trade law, any package

358
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:29,920
valued at $800 or less could enter the

359
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:32,160
country without formal customs entry.

360
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:34,440
That means no detailed inspection, no

361
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:36,680
duty, no paperwork beyond a basic

362
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:39,240
declaration. The rule was designed for

363
00:13:36,680 --> 00:13:40,760
personal gifts and small purchases.

364
00:13:39,240 --> 00:13:42,840
Things like someone ordering a book from

365
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:45,280
overseas, but it became one of the most

366
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:47,080
exploited pathways for counterfeit goods

367
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:49,480
on the planet. Because instead of

368
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:51,280
loading a container with 10,000 pairs of

369
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:53,240
fakes and rolling the dice at a customs

370
00:13:51,280 --> 00:13:55,720
checkpoint, a counterfeiter could ship

371
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:58,000
10,000 individual packages.

372
00:13:55,720 --> 00:14:00,800
Each one containing a single pair, each

373
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:02,800
one valued at under $800, and each one

374
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:04,800
slipping through under the threshold.

375
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:07,000
The volume of these small shipments was

376
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:09,040
enormous. Millions of de minimis

377
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,320
packages entered the United States every

378
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:13,080
year. And customs agents had neither the

379
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:15,160
staffing nor the infrastructure to

380
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:16,840
inspect more than a tiny fraction of

381
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:19,240
them. If you're wondering how that's

382
00:14:16,840 --> 00:14:21,440
even legal, so is everyone else.

383
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:24,280
Congress finally suspended the section

384
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:27,200
321 exemption for commercial shipments

385
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:29,160
is effective August 2025. Closing the

386
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:31,720
loophole after years of pressure from

387
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:33,760
brand owners and enforcement agencies.

388
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:35,560
But even with that loophole closing, the

389
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:37,000
container method still works. And the

390
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:38,640
numbers tell you how much is getting

391
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:43,120
through versus how much is getting

392
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:46,080
caught. In 2024, US Customs seized about

393
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:48,440
$127 million worth of counterfeit

394
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:50,240
footwear. That sounds like a lot until

395
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:52,560
you compare it to a $500 billion

396
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:54,520
industry. The enforcement agencies are

397
00:14:52,560 --> 00:14:56,640
catching a fraction of a fraction. And

398
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:58,200
the operations replace themselves faster

399
00:14:56,640 --> 00:15:00,400
than the paperwork on the seizure can be

400
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:01,880
filed. What comes next is the part that

401
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:04,360
still doesn't make sense to a lot of

402
00:15:01,880 --> 00:15:06,520
people. Because once these shoes land in

403
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:08,480
the United States, some of them don't

404
00:15:06,520 --> 00:15:10,640
end up on a street corner or a sketchy

405
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:12,640
website. They end up on platforms that

406
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:14,600
stake their entire business model on the

407
00:15:12,640 --> 00:15:17,400
promise that every shoe they sell is

408
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:19,440
real. The obvious victim is Nike. And

409
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:21,760
the numbers back that up. An estimated

410
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:23,480
10% of all Nike branded products sold

411
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:27,080
globally are counterfeit. That

412
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:29,480
translates to roughly $3.6 billion a

413
00:15:27,080 --> 00:15:31,360
year in lost revenue, money that goes to

414
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:34,040
the counterfeit pipeline instead of to

415
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:36,040
Nike and its authorized retailers. For a

416
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:38,280
company that generates tens of billions

417
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:40,600
in annual revenue, that's not a rounding

418
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:42,240
error. That's an entire product lines

419
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:44,600
worth of sales disappearing into a

420
00:15:42,240 --> 00:15:46,760
shadow market every single year. But

421
00:15:44,600 --> 00:15:49,080
Nike's losses are only one layer of who

422
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:50,800
pays. The second layer is an entire

423
00:15:49,080 --> 00:15:52,440
industry that exists solely because

424
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:55,720
counterfeits are good enough to fool

425
00:15:52,440 --> 00:15:58,000
people. Companies like StockX, GOAT, and

426
00:15:55,720 --> 00:16:00,600
eBay have all built authentication

427
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,400
services, employing teams of specialists

428
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:03,840
whose full-time job is to examine

429
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:06,840
sneakers and determine whether they're

430
00:16:03,840 --> 00:16:08,720
real. Work that only needs to exist

431
00:16:06,840 --> 00:16:10,640
because counterfeit sneakers are now

432
00:16:08,720 --> 00:16:13,680
manufactured at a level that regular

433
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:15,720
buyers and sometimes even experts cannot

434
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:18,960
distinguish from the real thing. In

435
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:21,480
2024, StockX reported that it rejected

436
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:23,800
more than 30,000 sneakers suspected of

437
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:26,000
being counterfeit. The total estimated

438
00:16:23,800 --> 00:16:29,120
value of those rejected shoes was nearly

439
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:32,839
$10 million. And StockX publishes a

440
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:35,000
number they're proud of, a 99.95%

441
00:16:32,839 --> 00:16:37,280
authentication accuracy rate. That

442
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:40,080
sounds almost perfect. And in a lot of

443
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:41,920
contexts, 99.95%

444
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:44,160
would be outstanding. But here's where

445
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:46,200
the math turns uncomfortable. StockX

446
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:50,120
processes millions of transactions a

447
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:51,240
