526
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:47,200
letting fakes through. That's a major

527
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:49,320
resale platform being held legally

528
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:51,400
responsible for selling counterfeits,

529
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:53,520
which sends a message to every platform

530
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:55,000
in the space. And then there's the case

531
00:19:53,520 --> 00:19:57,080
that might end up being the most

532
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:58,880
important one. Not because of the dollar

533
00:19:57,080 --> 00:20:00,600
amount, but because of what the jury

534
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:03,920
decided about how far trademark

535
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:06,400
protection actually reaches. In 2024, a

536
00:20:03,920 --> 00:20:09,040
federal jury in Los Angeles awarded Nike

537
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:11,160
$11 million against a fashion influencer

538
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:13,560
named Nicholas Tu Win En Berg, who ran a

539
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:14,960
brand called Divide the Youth. Tu Win En

540
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:16,880
Berg had been selling counterfeit

541
00:20:14,960 --> 00:20:19,040
sneakers through social media. But

542
00:20:16,880 --> 00:20:21,240
before he sold them, he removed the Nike

543
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:22,880
logos from the shoes. His argument was

544
00:20:21,240 --> 00:20:25,480
essentially that if you take the swoosh

